course: everyday
lesson: 325
title: Outdoors: icy playgrounds, blisters, cliff edges, trail markers, quarries, campfire burns, river crossings, bridges and pool safety
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Outdoors, small things are best dealt with early, and some places are never worth the risk: keep off icy playground equipment, tell a grown-up about a blister early, and stay well back from cliff edges and quarries.
explain: On a hike, learn the trail markers and check them at every junction, so you stay on the right path and can find your way back.
explain: A burn from a campfire should go under cool running water for 20 minutes, and a grown-up should be told; ice or butter make a burn worse.
explain: Water needs the most care of all: cross rivers only on a bridge or with a grown-up who says it is safe, never jump from bridges, walk at the pool, never push or hold anyone under, and never go in or near a pool without a grown-up watching.
explain: Example: a family in Colorado reaches a viewpoint with a fence. A boy wants to climb over for a better photo, but his mum explains that cliff edges can crumble without warning, so he takes the photo from behind the fence.
fact: Keep off playground equipment covered in ice; ice is very slippery and falls on it are hard.
fact: Tell a grown-up about a blister early and cover it with a plaster or blister pad, before it becomes an open sore.
fact: Stay well back from cliff edges and behind any fence, because edges can crumble and a fall can kill.
fact: Check trail markers or the map at every junction to stay on the right path; if you lose the trail, stop, check the map and go back to the last marker you saw.
fact: Stay away from quarries and old mines; rocks can fall and quarry water is very deep and cold.
fact: Cool a burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell a grown-up; never use ice or butter.
fact: Wet stepping stones and logs are slippery; cross a river only on a bridge or with a grown-up who says it is safe.
fact: Cross a bridge on the path, not on the railings, and never jump off it, because the water may hide rocks or be shallow.
fact: Walk, never run, around a pool, because wet tiles are slippery and falls cause head injuries.
fact: Never push, dunk or hold anyone under water, even in fun; people can hit the side, swallow water and drown.
fact: Never go in or near a pool without a grown-up watching; most child drownings happen in home pools when no one is watching.
exercise: The climbing frame in the park is covered in ice on a winter morning. What do you do? | Keep off it until the ice has gone | Climb carefully in your gloves | Slide down it on purpose for fun | Climb it if you hold on tight
exercise: Why is icy playground equipment dangerous? | Ice is very slippery and falls on it are hard | Ice makes the metal break | Ice makes the slide go too slowly, so you get stuck halfway down it | Cold metal gives you a fever
exercise: On a long walk your heel starts to rub and sting. What should you do? | Tell a grown-up early and put on a plaster or blister pad | Keep quiet and keep walking until the end, so you don't slow everyone down | Take off your shoes and walk barefoot | Pop it with a stick
exercise: Why should a rubbing heel be dealt with early on a hike? | A small rub left alone becomes a painful open sore | Blisters go away faster if you keep walking on them and let them toughen up | It only matters if it bleeds | Rubbing makes your boots wear out
exercise: You reach a cliff with an amazing view and a fence. Where do you stand? | Well back from the edge, behind the fence | On the very edge for the best photo | On the fence for a better view | Just past the fence, holding on to it
exercise: Why should you stay back from cliff edges even if the ground looks solid? | Edges can crumble without warning | Wind always blows people off cliffs | The view is better from further back | Cliffs are all private land, owned by farmers
exercise: You are hiking and come to a fork in the trail. What do you do? | Check the trail marker or the map before choosing | Take the path that looks shortest, because it will get you there quickest | Walk on and check later | Follow any other walker you see
exercise: You find an old flooded quarry with clear blue water on a hot day. What do you do? | Stay away and do not swim or climb there | Swim near the edge only | Jump in from a low rock | Climb the quarry walls for a view
exercise: Why is swimming in a quarry dangerous? | The water is very deep and cold and rocks can fall | Quarry water is always too shallow to swim in, so you hit the bottom | Quarries are full of sharks | The water is too warm to swim in
exercise: A spark from the campfire burns your hand. What do you do? | Cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell a grown-up | Rub butter on it straight away, to keep the air off the burn and soothe it | Hold an ice cube on it | Blow on it and carry on
exercise: A river has slippery stepping stones and no bridge. Your friend wants to cross. What do you say? | Only cross on a bridge or with a grown-up who says it is safe | Run across quickly from stone to stone so you do not have time to slip | Cross on the log instead, it is wider | Cross barefoot so you grip better
exercise: Some older children dare you to jump off a bridge into the river. What do you do? | Say no; there could be rocks or shallow water below | Jump in feet first, holding your nose, so it is safe | Jump only if they jump first | Walk along the railings instead
exercise: At the swimming pool, you are late for your lesson. How do you get to the water? | Walk, never run | Run on your toes so you do not slip | Run but slow down near the edge | Slide on the wet floor
exercise: Your friend is standing at the edge of the pool. You think it would be funny to push him in. What is true? | Never push anyone in; he could hit the side or swallow water | It is fine as long as the water is deep enough and he can't hit the bottom | It is fine if he is a good swimmer | It is fine if you both laugh
exercise: On holiday, the villa has a pool and the grown-ups are inside. What do you do? | Stay out of and away from the pool until a grown-up is watching | Swim only in the shallow end, where you can always stand up on the bottom | Swim if your friend is with you | Dip your feet at the edge alone
test: A boy in Minnesota sees that the slide is shiny with ice. His friend says it will be faster. What is wise? | Keep off it; icy equipment causes hard falls | Go down feet first so it is safe | Go down slowly holding the sides | Pour warm water on it first and go down at once
test: Halfway up a mountain trail, a girl's toe hurts but she says nothing. By the top it is bleeding. What should she have done? | Told a grown-up early so it could be covered | Walked faster to finish sooner | Kept her boots off for the rest of the walk | Tied her laces as tight as possible
test: Walkers in Cornwall see a sign: «Danger – unstable cliff edge». What does this mean for them? | The edge could crumble, so stay well back | They may stand on the edge for photos if they are careful and hold hands | It only matters in winter, when the ground is icy | It is safe for children because they are light
test: A family is lost in a forest after ignoring a junction sign. What could have kept them on track? | Checking the trail markers at every junction | Walking faster | Following a deer path | Going downhill at every fork
test: A boy burns his finger on a hot marshmallow stick. His friend says to put butter on it. What is true? | Butter makes it worse; cool it under running water for 20 minutes | Butter is the best thing for burns, because it soothes and seals the skin | Ice for an hour is better than water | A burn should be left alone and covered
test: A fallen tree lies across a river. A girl wants to walk across it to the other side. What is the danger? | The wet log is slippery and a fall into the river is dangerous | Logs always snap in the middle when someone walks across them, even thick ones | The river will push the log away at once | There is no danger if she goes slowly
test: Why is jumping from a bridge into a river dangerous, even if you can swim well? | The water may hide rocks or be shallow | Bridge water is always too cold to swim in | You might land on a boat that is always there | Swimmers always sink faster from a height
test: Where do most young children drown? | In home pools when no one is watching | In the middle of the sea on boat trips | In big public pools with lifeguards on duty | In the bath with an adult right beside them
test: Two cousins play «who can hold the other under longest» in the pool. What is true? | It is never safe; someone could swallow water and drown | It is fine as long as they count to ten and then let go | It is fine in the shallow end | It is fine because they are family
explain: A boy in Cornwall stood near a cliff edge to look at the sea; the edge crumbled and he fell, and he was rescued by helicopter with broken bones.
explain: Teenagers in England swam in a flooded quarry on a hot day; the water was icy cold underneath, and one of them drowned.
fact: Cliff edges can crumble without warning, and many people fall from them every year.
fact: Quarry water is very cold and deep and has hidden machinery and ledges, and people drown in it every summer.
test: cliff edge looks solid, let's sit right on it | Stay well back; edges can crumble | Sit down, it's solid rock and can't move | Sit there if you hold on to the grass | Lie down flat at the edge to look over
test: quarry pool looks so blue and nice, swim? | No, it's freezing and deep | Yes, it's lovely clean water | Yes, if you're a strong swimmer | Yes, but just near the edge
test: missed a trail marker and now dunno where we are | Stop, check the map and go back to the last marker | Keep walking forward | Split up to look | Run downhill
test: did u hear some teens jumped off the river bridge for fun and one "hit a rock under the water". lesson? | Never jump off a bridge; the water may hide rocks or be shallow | It's safe if the water looks deep and dark enough from up on the bridge | Jump feet first and it's fine | Only jump in summer
test: my mate says a girl slipped on the icy slide and "her head cracked open like an egg". what's the true bit | Keep off icy playground equipment; falls on ice are hard | Ice makes slides safer and faster | Only big kids fall on ice | Icy slides are fine with gloves
test: kids say the boy who got a blister on the hike "is soft". pass it on? | No, that's unkind; tell a grown-up early and cover it | Yes, he's soft | Yes, and laugh at his limp | Yes, it is just true, real hikers never get blisters
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hazards of quarry water? | It is very cold and deep, with hidden machinery and ledges | It is warm and shallow in summer, and therefore suitable for paddling | It is purified and safe for swimming | It is dangerous only during rain
exam: Identify the principal reason for which children must never be in or near a pool without a grown-up watching. | Most child drownings happen in home pools when no one is watching | Pools are always closed by law whenever no adult is present at the house | Children cannot open pool gates | Adults must check the temperature
exam: It is given that a river must be crossed. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | Only by a bridge, or with a grown-up who says it is safe | By stepping on wet stones quickly | By walking along a fallen log | By wading where it looks calm
exam: State the correct treatment for a minor burn from a campfire. | Cool running water for twenty minutes and telling a grown-up | Ice applied directly to the skin, held there until the pain stops | Butter spread over the burn | Leaving it uncovered in the sun
